{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Yes -- the Worthington-Sioux Falls branch (see chicago_stpaul_minneapolis_omaha.geojson) connected to a major through route, but that route wasn't otherwise represented on this map, which is why the branch looked isolated. The St. Paul and Sioux City Railroad (originally chartered 1864 as the Minnesota Valley Railroad Company, renamed 1869) built southwest from St. Paul down the Minnesota River valley through Mendota, Shakopee, Le Sueur, and Mankato, then out onto the prairie: St. James was reached by 1871, Worthington that same year (58 miles from St. James), and Le Mars, Iowa by 1872 (64 miles from Worthington). Through service St. Paul-Sioux City began Oct 1 1872, the final short Le Mars-Sioux City stretch using trackage that became Illinois Central's. So by the time the Worthington & Sioux Falls Railroad was built north from Worthington starting 1876 (see the other file), Worthington was already a junction on an established St. Paul-Sioux City trunk line, not a dead end. The Chicago, St. Paul, Minneapolis and Omaha Railway ('Omaha Road') bought the St. Paul and Sioux City in 1881, then was itself absorbed into the Chicago and North Western in 1882 -- giving the whole system, Sioux Falls branch included, a through connection to Chicago via the C&NW. Geometry is a real-town waypoint approximation (St. Paul-Mendota-Shakopee-Le Sueur-Mankato-St. James-Worthington-Le Mars-Sioux City), straight-line segments between documented stations -- NOT digitized from period track maps.", "sources": ["https://archive.org/stream/historyofstpauls00bishrich/historyofstpauls00bishrich_djvu.txt (Minnesota Valley Railroad Company incorporated Mar 4 1864, built St. Paul down the Minnesota River valley via Mendota, Shakopee, Le Sueur, Mankato; renamed St. Paul and Sioux City Railroad Apr 7 1869)", "https://www.dot.state.mn.us/culturalresources/docs/rail/sectione.pdf and https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worthington_Subdivision (St. James-Worthington, 58 miles, completed 1871; Worthington-Le Mars IA, 64 miles, completed 1872; through St. Paul-Sioux City service began Oct 1 1872, final leg into Sioux City over what became Illinois Central trackage)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago,_St._Paul,_Minneapolis_and_Omaha_Railway (Omaha Road bought the St. Paul and Sioux City 1881; Omaha Road itself absorbed into Chicago and North Western 1882, giving the system a through Chicago connection)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency for any era, all estimated; precise station list beyond the well-documented town-to-town mileposts; whether any named trains served this corridor before the Omaha Road/C&NW era.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "St. Paul and Sioux City Railroad", "line": "St. Paul and Sioux City: St. Paul \u2013 Sioux City", "id": "stpsc-stpaul-worthington-siouxcity", "color": "#E3B23C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 1870s steam-era prairie railroad; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1872, "end": 1881, "headway_min": 480, "note": "St. James-Worthington completed 1871, Worthington-Le Mars IA 1872; through St. Paul-Sioux City service began Oct 1 1872; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https:/